#### Perturbative QCD (pQCD)

Physics quantities, such as cross sections, can be calculated by a truncated power series with respect to the running coupling constant \(\alpha_{s}\) (\(\alpha_{s}^{n+2}\)), at sufficiently high momentum transfer scale \(\mu\), where the \(\alpha_{s}\) is sufficiently small. The lowest order term (\(n=0\)), which is so called Leading Order (LO) term, is with largest contributions to the physics process. For higher order term with \(n=1\)and \(n=2\) are called Next-to-Leading-Order (NLO) and Next-Next-to-Leading-Order (NNLO), respectively. Those higher order terms (\(n\geq 1\)) usually have small contributions and more complicated Feynman diagrams. There are plenty of experiments on high energy processes which can provide quantitative tests of pQCD.

The calculations are more complicated and difficult when pQCD is applied at the hadron level due to the non-perturbative nature of hadron structure. The so called QCD factorization theorem has been devoleped to calculate the cross section on hadron level, which separate the cross section into 2 parts: the process dependent parton cross section calculated by pQCD, and the universal long distance functions. For example, by assuming factorization, we can calculate the cross section of a process \(A+B\to C+...\) as:

\[\sigma_{AB\to C}=f_{a/A}(x_{a},\mu_{F}^{2})f_{b/B}(x_{b},\mu_{F}^{2}) \otimes\hat{\sigma}_{ab\to c}(\hat{s},\mu_{F}^{2},\mu_{R}^{2},\alpha_{s}) \otimes D_{c\to C}(z,\mu_{F}^{2}) \tag{1.3}\]

Figure 1.1: Left: Summary of measurements of \(\alpha_{s}(M_{\rm Z})\) from different experiments; Right: \(\alpha_{s}\) as a function of the energy scale \(\mu\) obtained from Lattice QCD calculations (shadow lines) and experiments (markers). The figures are taken from [7].

