## Appendix A Derivation of Statistical Error Formula

The latest error estimation used in moment analysis can be found in ref. [114]. The following analytical error propagation formula has been proved to be overestimating the statistic errors of the moments.

The definition of various moments \((M,\sigma,S,\kappa)\) can be written as :

\[M = <N>=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}N_{i}\] (A.1) \[\sigma = \sqrt{<(N-<N>)^{2}>}\] (A.2) \[S = \frac{<(N-<N>)^{3}>}{\sigma^{3}}\] (A.3) \[\kappa = \frac{<(N-<N>)^{4}>}{\sigma^{4}}-3\] (A.4)

where random variable \(N\) represents the measured quantity, \(n\) is the number of events in the event ensemble, \(N_{i}\) is the measured quantities for \(i^{th}\) event.

The Standard error propagation formula can be expressed as **:**

\[f = f(x_{1},x_{2}...x_{n})\] (A.5) \[\sigma(f) = \sqrt{\sum_{i=1}^{n}{(\frac{\partial f}{\partial x_{i}})^{2} \sigma_{x_{i}}^{2}}}\] (A.6)

The random variable \(f\) depends on other independent random variables \(x_{1},x_{2}...x_{n}\) and their errors are \(\sigma(f)\), \(\sigma_{x_{1}},\sigma_{x_{2}}...\sigma_{x_{n}}\), respectively.

### Mean (\(M\))

The derivative of the mean value (\(M\)) with respected to the \(N_{i}\):

\[\frac{\partial M}{\partial N_{i}}=\frac{\partial<N>}{\partial N_{i}}=\frac{1}{n}\] (A.7)

Then we can get the error formula of the mean value :

\[\sigma_{M}=\sqrt{\sum_{i=1}^{N}{(\frac{\partial<N>}{\partial N_{i}})^{2}\sigma _{N_{i}}^{2}}}=\frac{1}{n}\sqrt{\sum_{i=1}^{N}{\sigma_{N_{i}}^{2}}}\] (A.8)

where \(\sigma_{M}\) is the error of mean value and the \(\sigma_{N_{i}}\) is the error of the \(i^{th}\) measurements for the variable \(N\).

### Standard Deviation (\(\sigma\))

The derivative of the standard deviation (\(\sigma\)) with respected to the \(N_{i}\):

\[\frac{\partial\sigma}{\partial N_{i}}=\frac{1}{n\sigma}(N_{i}-<N>)\] (A.9)

Then we can get the error formula of the standard deviation :

\[\sigma_{std}=\sqrt{\sum_{i=1}^{N}{(\frac{\partial\sigma}{\partial N_{i}})^{2} \sigma_{N_{i}}^{2}}}=\frac{1}{n}\sqrt{\sum_{i=1}^{N}{\{(\frac{N_{i}-<N>}{ \sigma})^{2}\sigma_{N_{i}}^{2}\}}}\] (A.10)

where \(\sigma_{std}\) is the error of standard deviation and the \(\sigma_{N_{i}}\) is the error of the \(i^{th}\) measurements for the variable \(N\).

